---
import HoldingsPanel from '../../components/HoldingsPanel.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { fxNote, type Currency } from '../../lib/currency';
import { currencyPaths } from '../../lib/routes';

export const getStaticPaths = currencyPaths;

const { currency } = Astro.props as { currency: Currency };
---

<Base
  currency={currency}
  title="blockplot · holdings"
  description="Value your own BTC against the committed dataset. Stored only in your browser — the site is static and has no server to send it to."
>
  <h1 class="visually-hidden">Holdings</h1>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Your holdings · stored only in this browser</h2>
    <p class="method-note privacy-note">
      <strong>This stays on your device.</strong> What you enter is written to this browser's{' '}
      <code>localStorage</code> and read back by this page. It is never transmitted: blockplot is a
      static site with no server, no account, and no analytics, so there is nowhere for it to go
      even in principle. Every page — this one included — asks CoinGecko for the live BTC price
      about once a minute while the tab is visible, to keep the header ticker current, and the
      network page also asks mempool.space for fee tiers. Neither request carries anything you
      enter here.
      Both still reveal what any web request does: your IP address and the fact that you loaded
      blockplot. Clearing your browser data, or the button below, removes what you entered. Anyone with access to this browser profile can read it, so treat it as you would a
      note kept on the same machine.
    </p>
    <HoldingsPanel currency={currency} />
    <p class="method-note">
      Value is your BTC at the latest committed daily close, not a live price — the header ticker
      is the live figure. That close is CoinGecko's; the history chart is blockchain.com's, and the
      two disagree by a fraction of a percent on any given day, so the last point on the chart is
      not quite the figure above it. Each is labelled with its source rather than quietly
      reconciled. Profit and loss compare that value against the total you entered, so it
      is a simple return on money in, ignoring fees you have already paid, taxes, and anything you
      have since sold. A cost entered in the other currency is converted at the rate implied by
      today's two closes, which is today's rate rather than the one you actually paid — unless the
      two currencies' datasets are a different age, in which case that ratio is not a rate at all
      and the cost is held back rather than converted. It stays stored either way.{fxNote(currency)}
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
